1
00:00:06,057 --> 00:00:07,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Any specific questions?

2
00:00:08,399 --> 00:00:10,861
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's a few things I want to talk to you about.

3
00:00:10,881 --> 00:00:11,161
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

4
00:00:12,922 --> 00:00:19,968
[SPEAKER_00]: There's one about assuming the senses.

5
00:00:21,729 --> 00:00:22,750
[SPEAKER_00]: Assuming the senses.

6
00:00:25,292 --> 00:00:32,338
[SPEAKER_00]: If one assumes the senses, then, on account of that, feelings grow, and the mind becomes

7
00:00:35,152 --> 00:00:46,139
[SPEAKER_00]: The mind becomes damaged with covetousness, cruelty, and acts... and, yeah, this is just a heaping up of suffering.

8
00:00:46,619 --> 00:00:48,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That one is said to be far from nibbāna.

9
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:58,507
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when seeing a sight, mindfulness becomes confused.

10
00:00:59,908 --> 00:01:03,810
[SPEAKER_00]: If attention is focused on the pleasant aspect,

11
00:01:07,172 --> 00:01:10,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing this, one remains assuming.

12
00:01:10,795 --> 00:01:11,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

13
00:01:11,615 --> 00:01:22,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, basically, mindfulness gets confused if one attends pleasant aspect, which is the feelings that have arose based on the assumed senses.

14
00:01:22,944 --> 00:01:30,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, through simply attending that pleasure, that's the result of that assumption, you're actually maintaining that assumption through that thinking.

15
00:02:05,988 --> 00:02:14,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, there is... there is a... you can say the assumption comes first, but not temporally first, but structurally first.

16
00:02:16,131 --> 00:02:23,733
[SPEAKER_01]: The simultaneous presence of the assumption and the feelings that result on account of that assumption, but it's simultaneous.

17
00:02:23,833 --> 00:02:26,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you assume without feeling and then the feeling comes.

18
00:02:26,713 --> 00:02:37,256
[SPEAKER_01]: And because it's simultaneous, simply through attending feelings, you're maintaining that which sort of determines those feelings, which is an assumption.

19
00:02:38,796 --> 00:02:42,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And at the same time, your mind, eh... well, they're suffering.

20
00:02:43,574 --> 00:02:45,816
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of that whole picture, suffering is present.

21
00:02:45,836 --> 00:02:50,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But even that is again the same principle of that simultaneous presence.

22
00:02:53,321 --> 00:03:02,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can say assumption results in feelings, or assumption results in suffering, or assumption results in craving, or craving results in...

23
00:03:02,788 --> 00:03:04,350
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just simultaneously presence.

24
00:03:04,370 --> 00:03:09,814
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just maintaining each other, because you're attending any of it, in that sense.

25
00:03:10,254 --> 00:03:11,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the principle gets maintained.

26
00:03:15,315 --> 00:03:16,415
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, yeah.

27
00:03:16,716 --> 00:03:18,136
[SPEAKER_00]: Mind seems to be confused.

28
00:03:19,237 --> 00:03:19,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Equals.

29
00:03:24,499 --> 00:03:25,539
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what is...

30
00:03:25,619 --> 00:03:27,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, assuming the senses means?

31
00:03:27,860 --> 00:03:37,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, assuming the senses means... means... well, literally, assuming those senses to be yours, or to be for you.

32
00:03:37,525 --> 00:03:45,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, assuming senses to be yours, and then assuming that which you gather through those senses to be for you.

33
00:03:46,351 --> 00:03:51,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you assume that the sights you see are the sights that your eyes see.

34
00:03:51,655 --> 00:03:54,197
[SPEAKER_01]: The sights are yours, like for you, they're yours.

35
00:03:54,758 --> 00:04:00,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're looking at things that are not necessarily yours, the fact that you're looking with your eyes, it's like your sight.

36
00:04:02,184 --> 00:04:04,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that sight is for me, any sight of anything.

37
00:04:07,428 --> 00:04:08,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to start seeing

38
00:04:10,617 --> 00:04:15,341
[SPEAKER_01]: The dead phenomenon of senses, of my senses, it's kind of always a negative.

39
00:04:15,361 --> 00:04:20,705
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't actually see your eye with the same eye, in the same sense.

40
00:04:21,565 --> 00:04:28,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can... you end up assuming... well, you end up assuming because the senses are ambiguous.

41
00:04:28,771 --> 00:04:29,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't really know.

42
00:04:29,531 --> 00:04:31,012
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a big kind of dark

43
00:04:33,086 --> 00:04:35,688
[SPEAKER_01]: A vague cloud of all senses.

44
00:04:36,148 --> 00:04:40,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, all you have is sights, sounds, melted such as for you.

45
00:04:40,591 --> 00:04:42,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you just take it for granted.

46
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:56,640
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you discern long enough, through that repetition, that whatever notion of your senses you might have, that those senses are... Oh, I think my senses are...

47
00:04:57,440 --> 00:04:59,582
[SPEAKER_01]: If you discern that whatever notion you might have,

48
00:05:00,463 --> 00:05:19,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Those senses will never ever be anything more, and certainly will never be anything separable from a fleshy organ of eye, skin, tongue, like literally these organs, random objects in the world, that are put together in the unit of this body.

49
00:05:21,728 --> 00:05:29,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't examine the organs like a doctor would, impersonally, because then it doesn't work, then you don't see that connection.

50
00:05:30,919 --> 00:05:32,940
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see the dependence.

51
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:45,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to see your own organs and the only way you can see it is in that external sense of, oh, a lump of flesh, which is my eye, tongue, ears, eardrums.

52
00:05:45,246 --> 00:05:47,147
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just organs, literally.

53
00:05:47,768 --> 00:05:57,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Just as you have this bin, broom, stone, tree, eye, liquid, matter,

54
00:05:57,979 --> 00:05:58,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Brain.

55
00:05:58,840 --> 00:05:59,280
[SPEAKER_01]: Brain.

56
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:00,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, four elements.

57
00:06:01,662 --> 00:06:11,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But, these particular material organs are that because of which there are sights that you can discern in the world.

58
00:06:12,409 --> 00:06:15,051
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not in you, nor is it for you.

59
00:06:16,112 --> 00:06:21,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Those organs are external, as external as the external objects around you.

60
00:06:21,196 --> 00:06:23,718
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just, you don't see the

61
00:06:24,953 --> 00:06:25,533
[SPEAKER_01]: The connection.

62
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:27,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And that connection literally needs to be fabricated.

63
00:06:28,895 --> 00:06:31,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you will never see the connection naturally.

64
00:06:31,516 --> 00:06:36,418
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a secondary... Because natural... natural way is the way of ignorance.

65
00:06:37,218 --> 00:06:39,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Natural way is the way that goes with the grain.

66
00:06:40,619 --> 00:06:42,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to go against the grain.

67
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:43,180
[SPEAKER_01]: And basically...

68
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:49,903
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you were to dwell on it long enough, like a fabricating contemplation that your senses

69
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:57,723
[SPEAKER_01]: When I say my senses, whatever vague thought you might have about it, take it as vague.

70
00:06:57,803 --> 00:06:58,863
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to define it.

71
00:06:59,683 --> 00:07:17,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Take it as a whole phenomenon, as an entity, that vague thought of my senses, and then see the presence of that as directly determined, inseparable from the fleshy eye, the tongue, the skin, the nose, ears, brain, whatever.

72
00:07:18,588 --> 00:07:37,163
[SPEAKER_01]: You literally, like, no matter what vague thoughts I might have about my senses, the fact that I can't even think that ambiguously, abstractly, is because this living matter of my body, of this sixth sense base, is present in that material form.

73
00:07:37,903 --> 00:07:40,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when it gets destroyed, I can't think out of it.

74
00:07:41,426 --> 00:07:42,227
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

75
00:07:43,307 --> 00:07:55,213
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how much your mind, your thoughts, your ideal senses might feel different from the material objects, fundamentally, it can never, like, escape it.

76
00:07:55,233 --> 00:07:57,634
[SPEAKER_01]: So, destruction of matter will bring an end to this.

77
00:07:58,955 --> 00:08:12,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why we're realizing that no matter how much you volitionally, reflexively develop, you know, your thoughts, and your discernments, and your abstractions, and your reflect... whatever, whatever you want,

78
00:08:13,961 --> 00:08:21,446
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all there possible, because there is a living sixth sense body present in the world, as a matter.

79
00:08:22,927 --> 00:08:30,012
[SPEAKER_01]: When that matter gets destroyed, when that material is disintegrated, you can't be conceiving and abstracting in regard to it.

80
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:32,434
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't know any mental phenomenon.

81
00:08:32,454 --> 00:08:41,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter what intentionality you can have with your mind, it's subordinate to the presence of that gratuitous matter that is living.

82
00:08:42,048 --> 00:08:43,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is your body, which is your life.

83
00:08:43,809 --> 00:08:47,610
[SPEAKER_01]: When that's taken away, nothing can be left, nothing can remain.

84
00:08:47,890 --> 00:08:48,850
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just inconceivable.

85
00:08:50,651 --> 00:08:58,354
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like contemplating, like drilling, literally drilling that connection, because it's not obvious.

86
00:08:58,854 --> 00:09:00,554
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems fabricated, it seems abstract.

87
00:09:01,155 --> 00:09:02,275
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is, for Putrajana.

88
00:09:02,675 --> 00:09:03,715
[SPEAKER_01]: It is completely abstract.

89
00:09:04,956 --> 00:09:06,196
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the right abstraction.

90
00:09:06,496 --> 00:09:10,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the abstraction that, if repeated a sufficient number of times,

91
00:09:11,592 --> 00:09:17,436
[SPEAKER_01]: It will, like, take root, it will correspond to how things actually are.

92
00:09:17,856 --> 00:09:20,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can see how things actually are.

93
00:09:20,878 --> 00:09:25,681
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is that, yes, your senses cannot even be conceived to be yours.

94
00:09:26,202 --> 00:09:26,842
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible.

95
00:09:28,103 --> 00:09:31,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Let alone that which you acquire through your senses to be for you.

96
00:09:41,408 --> 00:09:52,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see what I mean?

97
00:09:54,057 --> 00:10:06,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you think now, or if anybody thinks, oh, my, like, my eyes, what is my, my eyes and senses, you immediately assume as inside of you, as internal, as part of you.

98
00:10:08,246 --> 00:10:09,447
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, that's a thought.

99
00:10:12,006 --> 00:10:25,335
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can have just another thought, which is, well, another way of seeing, which is seeing those objects as external, as a thought, because... Well, that's the thing.

100
00:10:25,355 --> 00:10:27,676
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always going to have a thought, nothing else.

101
00:10:28,037 --> 00:10:30,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when I said, ah, that's a thought, that doesn't mean I'm dismissing it.

102
00:10:30,598 --> 00:10:34,761
[SPEAKER_01]: I just meant that that big is that you think is the senses.

103
00:10:34,901 --> 00:10:40,305
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see, no, that's a thought that, obviously, for me, stands for that which I think senses are.

104
00:10:41,445 --> 00:10:55,928
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you start recognizing that all you're going to have is a phenomenon of thought in regard to your senses, then you realize, well, then, thinking these external organs, it's nothing less real than this thought, because that's also thought.

105
00:10:56,028 --> 00:10:56,989
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's the right thought.

106
00:10:57,209 --> 00:10:57,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

107
00:10:58,109 --> 00:11:10,452
[SPEAKER_01]: And then somebody tells you, somebody else, like the Buddha and the Suttas, tells you that that's the right thought, and if you keep repeating it, it will correspond to how things are, because it's right, and then you can see how things are.

108
00:11:11,541 --> 00:11:15,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But not without a sufficient amount of repetition.

109
00:11:18,884 --> 00:11:20,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's... that's...

110
00:11:20,044 --> 00:11:21,365
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we spoke about it before.

111
00:11:21,885 --> 00:11:28,987
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... that's why you can't stumble upon āyur-mantra by accident.

112
00:11:30,368 --> 00:11:34,309
[SPEAKER_01]: And, oh, yeah, I got it, just in a, you know, instantaneously.

113
00:11:34,369 --> 00:11:35,830
[SPEAKER_01]: I was doing this and then this happened.

114
00:11:36,030 --> 00:11:36,650
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't happen.

115
00:11:37,110 --> 00:11:41,112
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop it literally through training and drilling.

116
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:47,653
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, like in this instance, the Buddha tells you this is the right thought.

117
00:11:48,193 --> 00:11:52,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, experience of putusana is top to bottom, is completely wrong, affected by ignorance.

118
00:11:53,836 --> 00:11:56,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have a criteria of what is right thought, what it isn't.

119
00:11:57,258 --> 00:12:00,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you have to fit in the Buddha, and the Buddha tells this is the right thought.

120
00:12:00,700 --> 00:12:01,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can think that right thought.

121
00:12:02,541 --> 00:12:09,065
[SPEAKER_01]: But thinking it once won't make a difference, because you have this whole ocean of ignorance that needs to be drained away.

122
00:12:09,085 --> 00:12:11,146
[SPEAKER_01]: But then thinking it twice won't make a difference.

123
00:12:11,687 --> 00:12:13,249
[SPEAKER_01]: Thinking it three times won't make a difference.

124
00:12:13,609 --> 00:12:24,719
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep thinking it, like, five thousand times, then it will be drained in a way, and eventually, when it's officially drained, you'll recognize, oh, ignorance is drained.

125
00:12:26,041 --> 00:12:27,062
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so the point is...

126
00:12:28,173 --> 00:12:38,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That drilling, that doing... that in the beginning, yeah, you have to do it on account of faith, in the sense that, okay, you believe that the Buddha knew what he was talking about, so you have faith, so that's why you're applying that.

127
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:50,348
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why you can't do... like, you can sometimes, uh, accidentally, uh, stumble, like, do the right thing.

128
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:52,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Engage in the right thought.

129
00:12:52,762 --> 00:12:58,587
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't know that that is the right thought, if nobody told you, you will not know that that was the right thought.

130
00:12:58,927 --> 00:13:03,891
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will certainly not repeat it thousand of times without any apparent result.

131
00:13:04,391 --> 00:13:04,892
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because...

132
00:13:04,912 --> 00:13:06,173
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's impossible.

133
00:13:06,193 --> 00:13:12,198
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, by chance, you know, you can roll a dice and you can get it once and twice and five times.

134
00:13:12,218 --> 00:13:13,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't get it hundred.

135
00:13:13,079 --> 00:13:14,600
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... just impossible.

136
00:13:15,361 --> 00:13:18,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable to, like, always get the right result.

137
00:13:18,926 --> 00:13:21,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you know that that is the right result, then you always go for that.

138
00:13:22,847 --> 00:13:30,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why that utterance of another is one of the, like a, eh... fundamental factors for the right view.

139
00:13:31,629 --> 00:13:48,133
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot possibly, as I said, you might have been doing things rightly, attending things rightly, but if nobody externally told you that that is right thing, it's simply inconceivable that you would have stuck to repeating those things rightly

140
00:13:48,802 --> 00:13:53,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Thousands of times for years, because there is no apparent result right away.

141
00:13:53,986 --> 00:13:55,847
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you do it and you immediately see it.

142
00:13:56,468 --> 00:14:03,733
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to keep doing it, and doing it, and doing it, and doing it, and you accept it intellectually as the right thing, like reason.

143
00:14:04,033 --> 00:14:09,738
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said, oh, the man who wants to be free from suffering goes and, like, frequents the wise men.

144
00:14:09,778 --> 00:14:11,919
[SPEAKER_01]: He listens to what they say.

145
00:14:12,019 --> 00:14:15,262
[SPEAKER_01]: He ponders, makes sense, measures the task, then he does it.

146
00:14:16,649 --> 00:14:23,393
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you accept it through reasoning, through discernment on that level, but then you keep repeating it, and repeating, and repeating.

147
00:14:23,413 --> 00:14:33,938
[SPEAKER_01]: And because you had faith in what the Buddha said to be right, you stick to it, and you repeat it ten thousands of times, and because it was right, you become an arahant.

148
00:14:34,679 --> 00:14:43,623
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you didn't know, if you didn't have faith that it was right, you wouldn't have possibly stuck to it for that long, because there is no... there is no confirmation of it.

149
00:14:44,724 --> 00:14:46,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Until much, much later.

150
00:14:46,829 --> 00:14:47,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there's no confirmation.

151
00:14:50,414 --> 00:14:52,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have different results.

152
00:14:52,957 --> 00:14:57,283
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have pleasant results, you might have a lessening of suffering or more of an understanding.

153
00:14:57,464 --> 00:15:03,392
[SPEAKER_00]: What gives you the motivation to continue?

154
00:15:04,904 --> 00:15:12,473
[SPEAKER_00]: If you haven't had the end... you don't have the end result, but you... it makes sense what the Buddha says, it makes sense, so you follow the teaching.

155
00:15:13,334 --> 00:15:21,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Then... but you're not getting great results or... Well, that's... that's for somebody who has heard the teaching.

156
00:15:21,924 --> 00:15:22,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

157
00:15:22,385 --> 00:15:22,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

158
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:27,601
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that can be an inspiration for them to continue, even without the apparent results.

159
00:15:27,841 --> 00:15:28,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because it makes sense.

160
00:15:29,042 --> 00:15:39,845
[SPEAKER_01]: But imagine how impossible it is for somebody to continue, who hasn't even heard or accepted, through reasoning and developed faith, that what the Buddha said was right.

161
00:15:40,545 --> 00:15:46,627
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible for them to continue, because there is no... there is absolutely nothing changes through these drillings.

162
00:15:46,747 --> 00:15:48,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't see nothing, nothing better, no worse.

163
00:15:48,767 --> 00:15:50,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You just... you won't even notice it.

164
00:15:51,866 --> 00:15:54,688
[SPEAKER_01]: And let alone stick to it and repeat it thousands of times.

165
00:15:54,708 --> 00:15:58,891
[SPEAKER_01]: The right intention, the avoiding the wrong intention, and so on and so forth.

166
00:15:58,951 --> 00:16:00,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Attending to things rightly.

167
00:16:01,013 --> 00:16:01,153
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

168
00:16:01,173 --> 00:16:02,094
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be told.

169
00:16:02,614 --> 00:16:04,135
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're told, you can discern it.

170
00:16:04,395 --> 00:16:05,877
[SPEAKER_01]: When you discern it, then you can do it.

171
00:16:07,538 --> 00:16:12,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Because as you... yeah... as it's... something makes sense to you, so you do it.

172
00:16:13,943 --> 00:16:15,904
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, say for example, restraint.

173
00:16:16,464 --> 00:16:18,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Just, you know, keep a strength and aim.

174
00:16:19,628 --> 00:16:27,815
[SPEAKER_00]: He reflects, as he's doing it, he looks and he sees that there is a... the pleasure of being blameless.

175
00:16:29,056 --> 00:16:31,058
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's not he's an arahant, he's not a sotāpanna.

176
00:16:31,078 --> 00:16:31,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

177
00:16:31,538 --> 00:16:33,880
[SPEAKER_00]: But, ah, that thing is there.

178
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:39,465
[SPEAKER_01]: There are ways of developing wisdom without Buddha's aid, but obviously it will never lead to the

179
00:16:40,477 --> 00:16:45,459
[SPEAKER_01]: The final abandoning of the assumption of self and self-view and arahant.

180
00:16:45,519 --> 00:16:54,942
[SPEAKER_00]: This is what I mean, that once you actually heard the Buddha's teaching, and you do follow, you do restrain yourself, but you don't go that far.

181
00:16:54,962 --> 00:16:56,483
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know if you understood it rightly.

182
00:16:56,923 --> 00:17:03,425
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep the precepts and you get the joy that comes from that, then you can maybe be a little bit more confident.

183
00:17:04,026 --> 00:17:07,467
[SPEAKER_00]: You're thinking, oh yeah, there might be something more to this.

184
00:17:08,082 --> 00:17:13,385
[SPEAKER_00]: What he says after being restrained and the rest of it might also be true, so I'll continue on.

185
00:17:13,866 --> 00:17:21,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this... on top of that, also, as I said, like, you might be doing things rightly or not.

186
00:17:22,471 --> 00:17:23,031
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know.

187
00:17:23,972 --> 00:17:32,417
[SPEAKER_01]: And because you haven't been told externally, through the suttas and through the Buddha's instruction, what you should be doing,

188
00:17:32,784 --> 00:17:38,266
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't really have even the external criteria of what kind of corresponds to what what might be right.

189
00:17:39,266 --> 00:17:48,049
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but then, if you were to develop that criteria, then you can look back at the stuff you used to do, and you can actually, yeah, back then,

190
00:17:48,782 --> 00:17:53,146
[SPEAKER_01]: This such-and-such thing, such-and-such way of attending that I was doing, that was actually right.

191
00:17:53,306 --> 00:17:56,248
[SPEAKER_01]: It corresponds to the dhamma principles, so to speak.

192
00:17:57,069 --> 00:18:03,914
[SPEAKER_01]: It corresponds to how things... Obviously, back then, you didn't know what those principles were, so you couldn't have stuck to it and repeated it long enough.

193
00:18:03,934 --> 00:18:09,359
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't... you can do it by chance once, twice, ten times, but not ten thousands of times by chance.

194
00:18:09,579 --> 00:18:10,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

195
00:18:10,560 --> 00:18:13,302
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to have an anchor to bring you back, which will be the discernment.

196
00:18:14,337 --> 00:18:20,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can also look back and see, oh, when I thought I was right in practicing, I was completely wrong.

197
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:30,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now you've got the criteria that you can also see things in the past you used to do, your attempts at practice, and so on, and you can see, oh, actually, no, that was not that wrong.

198
00:18:31,383 --> 00:18:32,884
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, back then, you didn't know.

199
00:18:33,444 --> 00:18:38,826
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody told you back then, you would have stuck to it, but nobody told you, so it took you longer, or not, it depends.

200
00:18:39,667 --> 00:18:41,827
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's why it's... it's... it's not optional.

201
00:18:42,348 --> 00:18:43,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Alternance of another.

202
00:18:44,550 --> 00:18:49,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, obviously, means that's why it also needs to have that initial step, first step of faith.

203
00:18:50,594 --> 00:19:01,040
[SPEAKER_01]: That you have faith that this other, the Buddha, the suttas, the arahant, the sotāpanna, might be the Buddha, the sutta, the arahant, the sotāpanna, might know what they're talking about.

204
00:19:01,680 --> 00:19:06,744
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, it's not that that faith needs to stay till the end.

205
00:19:07,024 --> 00:19:09,105
[SPEAKER_01]: That faith is the initial push and then, you know,

206
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:11,760
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it rightly, you'll come to recognize.

207
00:19:12,321 --> 00:19:16,502
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you won't need faith, or rather, you'll replace the faith with confidence, because now you know.

208
00:19:17,022 --> 00:19:17,922
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the suttas.

209
00:19:18,782 --> 00:19:23,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Confirmed confidence in the Buddha, in the Dhamma, in the Saṅgha, replaces that faith in the Buddha.

210
00:19:23,703 --> 00:19:24,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you know.

211
00:19:24,443 --> 00:19:26,084
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to believe in it, because I know it.

212
00:19:27,164 --> 00:19:36,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... it's this... similar to... something makes sense, you don't know, but it makes the most sense, and then you understand.

213
00:19:36,066 --> 00:19:39,367
[SPEAKER_01]: But always, always in the back of your mind, eh...

214
00:19:40,227 --> 00:19:53,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Have a little bit of reserve, because as puthujjana, you don't have a criteria of what really makes... or rather, what makes sense to you as a puthujjana, does not necessarily mean it's the right thing.

215
00:19:54,392 --> 00:19:59,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because often, yeah, it feels right when people are like, oh, I know I'm not a Sotāpanna, but it feels right.

216
00:19:59,453 --> 00:20:01,954
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, that's a big mistake right there.

217
00:20:03,015 --> 00:20:04,175
[SPEAKER_01]: You know you don't know,

218
00:20:04,972 --> 00:20:10,074
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you're happy to follow what you think you know, while you at the same time say, I don't know.

219
00:20:11,395 --> 00:20:21,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're just like undermining yourself within the same sentence.

220
00:20:23,320 --> 00:20:29,803
[SPEAKER_00]: This connection with the Buddha says dhamma is leading onwards.

221
00:20:31,944 --> 00:20:33,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, anyone can read that.

222
00:20:33,364 --> 00:20:34,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And is that maybe not it?

223
00:20:37,723 --> 00:20:41,906
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, again, you need to discern.

224
00:20:42,566 --> 00:20:50,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, like, I'm sure that people who think dhamma leading, like, upanayaka means leading inwards, or leading onwards, or...

225
00:20:51,030 --> 00:20:54,272
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sure that they're convinced that that is what it means.

226
00:20:54,972 --> 00:21:03,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to be completely self-transparent and see, okay, so, do I really hold on to this belief just because it's pleasing to me?

227
00:21:05,021 --> 00:21:15,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Just because it suits my other beliefs, or is it because it really, really makes sense after, like, deep, profound, repetitive, tedious drilling, investigation and contemplation?

228
00:21:16,766 --> 00:21:32,193
[SPEAKER_01]: When you know that you have not, kind of, just covered it up, like, oh, no, no, no, I believe in this, it's fine, but you really drilled into it and there was nothing left unturned, then, okay, it's least likely to be wrong, because you really drilled through it, through the whole thing.

229
00:21:32,213 --> 00:21:33,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the attitude you always have to have.

230
00:21:34,802 --> 00:21:43,966
[SPEAKER_01]: But most often, people just maintain beliefs, because it helps them maintain their previous beliefs, helps them maintain their previous beliefs, and then, so it's just like maintenance of their beliefs.

231
00:21:44,846 --> 00:21:46,847
[SPEAKER_01]: And they all do that for one reason.

232
00:21:46,967 --> 00:21:49,348
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pleasing to me, as the sūtra say.

233
00:21:51,328 --> 00:21:55,930
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's... when it's, eh... what is leading onwards?

234
00:21:55,950 --> 00:21:57,451
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, leading onwards, it's exactly that.

235
00:21:57,691 --> 00:22:03,253
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you were to understand, for example, what dhamma is,

236
00:22:03,864 --> 00:22:07,827
[SPEAKER_01]: That understanding will be taking you further and further and further till the other end.

237
00:22:07,847 --> 00:22:10,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's leading you onwards through that understanding.

238
00:22:10,328 --> 00:22:10,529
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

239
00:22:10,649 --> 00:22:11,629
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a theory.

240
00:22:11,729 --> 00:22:12,870
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not an abstraction.

241
00:22:12,930 --> 00:22:15,732
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not something you can accept or reject.

242
00:22:16,372 --> 00:22:20,535
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something whereby, through understanding, you're going further.

243
00:22:20,976 --> 00:22:22,236
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you realize there is more.

244
00:22:23,017 --> 00:22:23,537
[SPEAKER_01]: There is more.

245
00:22:23,657 --> 00:22:26,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that understanding, through that criteria, there is more.

246
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:27,520
[SPEAKER_01]: There is more.

247
00:22:28,060 --> 00:22:33,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Until, as Arahants or when the Buddha described, says, ah, there is no beyond this.

248
00:22:34,323 --> 00:22:36,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's leading onwards, naturally.

249
00:22:36,905 --> 00:22:46,211
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... like, if dhamma is presented as an explanation that you can accept or not, take it or leave it, that's not leading onwards.

250
00:22:46,671 --> 00:22:47,712
[SPEAKER_01]: That's an explanation.

251
00:22:48,512 --> 00:22:49,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, done, right in that.

252
00:22:49,833 --> 00:22:56,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why often the common-player interpretations, they kind of lay it out, and then you just read it and say, OK, so that's how it is.

253
00:22:57,218 --> 00:23:00,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no panaika element in that, because, OK, that's how it is.

254
00:23:01,380 --> 00:23:01,981
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

255
00:23:03,328 --> 00:23:26,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha, whenever he would be, like in all of the suttas, and I think I told you that the other day, he never... like, no matter what approach he would take on in expounding dhamma to a person or to a group of people, he would follow that approach through to the final point of arhanty, freedom from suffering, whatever, even if those people don't necessarily achieve it, his instruction would go all the way.

256
00:23:26,859 --> 00:23:29,380
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't stop halfway through his

257
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:35,024
[SPEAKER_01]: approach that through understanding of nāmarūpa and the all, you can become an arahant and so on.

258
00:23:35,264 --> 00:23:43,590
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't stop halfway through and then say, well, as a matter of fact, nāmarūpa also corresponds to the five aggregates, and then start comparing it linearly.

259
00:23:44,471 --> 00:23:45,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's right.

260
00:23:45,772 --> 00:23:47,013
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can... it does correspond.

261
00:23:47,033 --> 00:23:52,717
[SPEAKER_01]: This experience as a whole, whether you take it as five aggregates or nāmarūpa-viññāṇa, viññāṇa-nāmarūpa,

262
00:23:54,275 --> 00:23:56,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this and that, literally.

263
00:23:57,137 --> 00:24:02,600
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start comparing it, it ceases to be that open icon, like, leading on.

264
00:24:03,440 --> 00:24:05,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it ceases to be dhamma.

265
00:24:06,042 --> 00:24:09,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Because dhamma is dhamma only when it leads you out of suffering, when it leads you towards enlightenment.

266
00:24:10,144 --> 00:24:13,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can be talking the dhamma, like, dhamma terms and stuff.

267
00:24:13,826 --> 00:24:15,087
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, it's not leading on.

268
00:24:16,207 --> 00:24:16,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the dhamma.

269
00:24:18,489 --> 00:24:19,849
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, like,

270
00:24:21,418 --> 00:24:27,905
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even, by accident, teach dhamma to somebody else, without you yourself understanding it.

271
00:24:29,687 --> 00:24:40,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that open-eyed element is not... it's something you can't... something you can understand and know, and then convey to another person, in the right term, knowing what, like,

272
00:24:40,855 --> 00:25:01,666
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, knowing what to say for them to understand, not sort of comparing dhamma and comparing the principles and the terminology for the sake, like, for the scholarly sake by itself, in itself, and then present it as a kind of, okay, here's a theory, here's my explanation, and it's like, okay, sure, I accept that, and that's it.

273
00:25:01,686 --> 00:25:02,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You leave it at that.

274
00:25:04,507 --> 00:25:10,050
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a person who understood, like an arahant or the Buddha, would know that, because that's what understanding is.

275
00:25:10,890 --> 00:25:13,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, they wouldn't be teaching as a form of explanation.

276
00:25:13,710 --> 00:25:14,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference.

277
00:25:14,551 --> 00:25:18,911
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're explaining something, ah, okay, it makes sense, that's it.

278
00:25:19,852 --> 00:25:22,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, when you're leading somebody onwards in their understanding.

279
00:25:26,973 --> 00:25:35,894
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sure, I could compare, like, if nāmarūpa, viññāṇa, vipassana, if, as a whole, that approach is leading you onwards.

280
00:25:37,255 --> 00:25:38,915
[SPEAKER_01]: But if we are just doing it for the sake of

281
00:25:40,286 --> 00:25:46,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Debate, like abstract reasoning, or whatever, just theory, or just because it's pleasing to me, that's just pointless.

282
00:25:47,111 --> 00:25:51,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if we actually talk about dhamma all the time, we're not, because there is no leading-on-ones element.

283
00:25:53,235 --> 00:26:04,422
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, you can see leading-on-ones, like when I said about the assumption of the senses, I could have said, well, see, that's because you think your senses are yours, and that's it.

284
00:26:04,542 --> 00:26:07,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I should think my senses are not, not mine, and that's it.

285
00:26:08,205 --> 00:26:08,385
[SPEAKER_01]: But

286
00:26:09,110 --> 00:26:12,052
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just linear and there is absolutely no...

287
00:26:12,072 --> 00:26:13,773
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I tell you, well, actually, you have to stop.

288
00:26:14,254 --> 00:26:29,324
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see that your senses, the thought of what you think your senses are, is inseparable from that material thought, again, of the fleshy eye and all of the other sense bases.

289
00:26:30,325 --> 00:26:34,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to keep drilling that connection on and on and on.

290
00:26:34,768 --> 00:26:36,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just once, okay, that this is not mine.

291
00:26:37,827 --> 00:26:40,608
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to discern it and see dependent upon that.

292
00:26:41,969 --> 00:26:53,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will have, like, if you abandon your senses, means then the feelings won't be generated, which means then the mindfulness will not be clouded, which means the suffering won't arise.

293
00:26:53,375 --> 00:26:54,216
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will feel that.

294
00:26:54,296 --> 00:26:55,956
[SPEAKER_01]: You will feel the absence of suffering.

295
00:26:56,997 --> 00:26:58,898
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, like,

296
00:26:59,405 --> 00:27:08,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, I could say to myself, senses are not mine, but it doesn't make sense, makes any no difference to the suffering that you experience or that you might experience.

297
00:27:08,990 --> 00:27:12,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering that you might be liable to, makes no difference.

298
00:27:13,632 --> 00:27:15,613
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it makes no difference, means it's not the dhamma.

299
00:27:16,534 --> 00:27:19,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're actually reading the suttas, but it's not the dhamma.

300
00:27:20,287 --> 00:27:35,195
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you attend to things rightly, and you undo the assumption of your senses, so, through that undoing, you are actually, like, not liable to that suffering anymore, and you can know that, you know, this is dhamma, because it's directly freeing you from suffering.

301
00:27:35,735 --> 00:27:45,800
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in order to be able to see that, to discern that, you need to be completely self-transparent, and not act out of this view is simply pleasing to me, that's why I'm holding it.

302
00:27:46,620 --> 00:27:49,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to be ready to have some very displeasing views,

303
00:27:50,716 --> 00:28:03,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Some views that will fill you with anxiety, and everything else that follows, because you're undoing, like, your own assumption of your own very existence, of your own sense of self, of your own confidence as a person, or as a whatever else.

304
00:28:04,380 --> 00:28:09,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You're undoing all of that, and using only criteria of absence of suffering.

305
00:28:10,161 --> 00:28:16,023
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see, like, oh, well, this, actually, I'm not just acting because it's pleasing, this is really the real thing.

306
00:28:16,323 --> 00:28:17,123
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, look at that.

307
00:28:18,736 --> 00:28:25,184
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to find out the next moment that that is not a real thing, would you be filled with terror and anxiety?

308
00:28:25,544 --> 00:28:25,905
[SPEAKER_01]: You would.

309
00:28:25,925 --> 00:28:28,288
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's not a real thing.

310
00:28:28,308 --> 00:28:28,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a real thing.

311
00:28:34,695 --> 00:28:41,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as I said, the approach that you took, like, like, oh, I'm gonna hold this view because it's pleasing to me.

312
00:28:41,640 --> 00:28:43,842
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like, okay, that's not good, that's not wholesome.

313
00:28:44,322 --> 00:28:47,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm gonna hold this view, but I'm not doing it because it's pleasing to me.

314
00:28:48,445 --> 00:28:48,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine?

315
00:28:48,946 --> 00:28:51,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be true, but then look at that.

316
00:28:51,468 --> 00:28:58,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Look whereby, if that view is taken away from you, or if you find out that it's wrong, would you be affected personally?

317
00:28:59,546 --> 00:29:00,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, thoroughly.

318
00:29:00,286 --> 00:29:01,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, yes, I would.

319
00:29:01,667 --> 00:29:01,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

320
00:29:02,267 --> 00:29:10,730
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that view might not be wrong, but I'm certainly holding on to it, because if that view is gone, I'll experience this anxiety and terror, which means I need to take it further.

321
00:29:11,271 --> 00:29:15,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in a way, you are still holding, because it's pleasing to you, even if it's the right view.

322
00:29:16,513 --> 00:29:27,877
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're holding a view regardless of it giving you pleasure or giving you pain,

323
00:29:29,682 --> 00:29:33,324
[SPEAKER_00]: But yet, it just... you can't see it any other way.

324
00:29:33,344 --> 00:29:38,826
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just... you know, it's... Well, first of all, you can, but not hold any views.

325
00:29:39,907 --> 00:29:44,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not... it's not... you can only go beyond them as an arahant.

326
00:29:45,229 --> 00:29:48,711
[SPEAKER_01]: But before that, you can only hold the right views on account of wrong views.

327
00:29:49,331 --> 00:29:51,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can hold the wrong views and not have any right views.

328
00:29:52,173 --> 00:29:56,275
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, the whole notion of, oh, I don't hold to any views, that's a view.

329
00:29:57,088 --> 00:29:59,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, there was that sutta that I'm actually referring to.

330
00:30:00,251 --> 00:30:04,494
[SPEAKER_01]: When he said, oh, he said, Buddha asked him, so what, what views do you have?

331
00:30:04,514 --> 00:30:08,377
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, ah, Master Gautama, I have, uh, what did he say?

332
00:30:09,818 --> 00:30:11,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, I have no, I have no...

333
00:30:11,259 --> 00:30:12,961
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, he asked him, what views are pleasing to you?

334
00:30:13,001 --> 00:30:15,403
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, no, Master Gautama, I don't accept any views.

335
00:30:15,763 --> 00:30:16,444
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no views.

336
00:30:16,684 --> 00:30:17,965
[SPEAKER_01]: No views are pleasing to me.

337
00:30:18,865 --> 00:30:21,488
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, well, surely that view is pleasing to you.

338
00:30:22,568 --> 00:30:24,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Not holding to any views, it's also a view.

339
00:30:25,457 --> 00:30:26,517
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to discern that.

340
00:30:26,577 --> 00:30:28,458
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, oh, just stop thinking.

341
00:30:28,478 --> 00:30:29,138
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not thinking.

342
00:30:29,158 --> 00:30:30,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a thought.

343
00:30:30,079 --> 00:30:32,199
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just contradicting yourself right there and then.

344
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:40,142
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, if you believe you're not holding to any views, you're kind of in limbo, hovering over, that's a view that you are clearly not aware of.

345
00:30:41,543 --> 00:30:43,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you don't become aware of it, you can't abandon it.

346
00:30:44,544 --> 00:30:46,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to recognize you have no choice but to hold the views.

347
00:30:47,865 --> 00:30:50,586
[SPEAKER_01]: What you can choose is develop the right views,

348
00:30:51,231 --> 00:30:52,432
[SPEAKER_01]: At the expense of wrong views.

349
00:30:52,792 --> 00:30:58,114
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, hold the right views, then, because they're right, they will take you eventually beyond the views.

350
00:30:58,354 --> 00:30:58,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

351
00:30:58,935 --> 00:31:00,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you become an arahant.

352
00:31:00,275 --> 00:31:02,336
[SPEAKER_01]: But even then, I'm not holding any views.

353
00:31:02,376 --> 00:31:02,576
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

354
00:31:02,676 --> 00:31:03,797
[SPEAKER_01]: I went beyond the views.

355
00:31:03,937 --> 00:31:09,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, holding or not holding, or both holding, and neither holding or not holding, simply doesn't apply anymore.

356
00:31:10,000 --> 00:31:10,860
[SPEAKER_01]: You dropped it all.

357
00:31:12,281 --> 00:31:14,962
[SPEAKER_00]: But, isn't that, eh, another sutta, thirty-eight?

358
00:31:15,382 --> 00:31:16,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Manjhima Nukkāna, isn't it?

359
00:31:17,504 --> 00:31:20,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh, this, this right view,

360
00:31:24,554 --> 00:31:26,415
[SPEAKER_00]: As pleasing as it is.

361
00:31:27,295 --> 00:31:29,416
[SPEAKER_00]: As pure, as bright.

362
00:31:30,017 --> 00:31:33,058
[SPEAKER_00]: If you were to hold on to this view, you would not be...

363
00:31:33,178 --> 00:31:33,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing.

364
00:31:33,978 --> 00:31:34,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing it.

365
00:31:35,019 --> 00:31:35,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

366
00:31:35,739 --> 00:31:37,720
[SPEAKER_00]: You would not understand the dhamma to be a raft.

367
00:31:38,220 --> 00:31:40,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

368
00:31:41,441 --> 00:31:50,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's, that's like, that's what, that's what would happen to, to ārya-sāvakas who were not, who didn't reach ārahantship.

369
00:31:50,908 --> 00:31:54,951
[SPEAKER_01]: who kind of got complacent, like sotāpanna, sākāni, nidāsutta.

370
00:31:55,291 --> 00:32:11,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, they got, you know, and yeah, the great mass of suffering has been destroyed and so on, and they're pleased with that, which means they won't be pushing their view further, they'll just hold on to it, because they're satisfied with what they got, basically, they got complacent.

371
00:32:13,628 --> 00:32:15,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what will happen.

372
00:32:15,750 --> 00:32:16,850
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, even the right view.

373
00:32:17,111 --> 00:32:19,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, oh, so that... no, that's not a bad thing.

374
00:32:20,213 --> 00:32:27,278
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it's a bad thing from... or rather, it's not a fully adequate thing from the point of view of an arahant, but from pratyūjana.

375
00:32:27,518 --> 00:32:34,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, please, by all means, everybody should try and get the right view and hold on to it daily, rather than the wrong view.

376
00:32:34,983 --> 00:32:36,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, actually, holding on to the right view

377
00:32:37,890 --> 00:32:43,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... well, you can... because it's a right view, because you have the right view, you have the right criteria, you can see that you're holding on to it.

378
00:32:44,918 --> 00:32:48,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can, like, not exert yourself in practice further, but you'll know.

379
00:32:49,263 --> 00:32:51,445
[SPEAKER_01]: It's particularly the danger is you don't know that.

380
00:32:51,705 --> 00:32:53,808
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't know that you are holding on to those views.

381
00:32:54,388 --> 00:32:57,852
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you don't know, there's absolutely no way you can abandon them.

382
00:32:58,373 --> 00:32:59,514
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's much more dangerous.

383
00:33:15,719 --> 00:33:19,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Going back to something making sense.

384
00:33:19,181 --> 00:33:20,282
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you have faith.

385
00:33:20,342 --> 00:33:22,524
[SPEAKER_00]: OK, that makes sense.

386
00:33:25,446 --> 00:33:26,927
[SPEAKER_00]: But there's always your own effort.

387
00:33:26,947 --> 00:33:29,308
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to... there's always this... your own effort.

388
00:33:30,369 --> 00:33:43,438
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even though it makes sense, you're also going to be authentic enough to see that there is no... even though this is pleasant, this idea, this view that this monk says, and I really believe what he says,

389
00:33:45,310 --> 00:33:54,812
[SPEAKER_00]: But I don't really experience what he says, because I haven't made that effort.

390
00:33:55,012 --> 00:33:56,793
[SPEAKER_00]: I haven't made that effort, so how can I really know?

391
00:33:57,053 --> 00:34:02,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Making that effort is when I say, ah, you need to do the drilling, on and on and on.

392
00:34:03,174 --> 00:34:09,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Drilling of the right contemplation, right attending, drilling of not tolerating unwholesome states, not tolerating sensual thoughts.

393
00:34:09,916 --> 00:34:13,077
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those are drilling, all this hard work, internal work.

394
00:34:13,866 --> 00:34:16,507
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, that's basically 99%.

395
00:34:16,947 --> 00:34:17,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to do that.

396
00:34:18,327 --> 00:34:23,769
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the same sense, well, what this such-and-such monk said, it makes sense.

397
00:34:24,069 --> 00:34:24,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you start doing it.

398
00:34:25,010 --> 00:34:27,390
[SPEAKER_01]: You start applying it, and then you actually know it's wrong.

399
00:34:27,810 --> 00:34:31,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will know why it's wrong, and where it's wrong, and how it's wrong.

400
00:34:32,732 --> 00:34:39,054
[SPEAKER_00]: There's also this kind of... you can actually see what to do.

401
00:34:40,594 --> 00:34:42,235
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that not only makes sense,

402
00:34:43,549 --> 00:34:46,772
[SPEAKER_00]: But you you're able to see what you have to do with the bud.

403
00:34:47,113 --> 00:34:47,453
[SPEAKER_00]: I guess.

404
00:34:47,473 --> 00:34:49,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you're leaning onwards.

405
00:34:49,555 --> 00:34:50,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

406
00:34:50,136 --> 00:34:51,197
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the other bit in the sutra.

407
00:34:51,237 --> 00:34:53,319
[SPEAKER_01]: It says he measures the task.

408
00:34:53,759 --> 00:34:54,460
[SPEAKER_01]: You know exactly

409
00:34:55,052 --> 00:34:55,512
[SPEAKER_01]: How much?

410
00:34:55,873 --> 00:34:56,914
[SPEAKER_01]: What needs to be done?

411
00:34:57,074 --> 00:34:57,534
[SPEAKER_01]: What needs?

412
00:34:57,694 --> 00:34:58,975
[SPEAKER_01]: The task that is measured.

413
00:34:59,175 --> 00:34:59,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

414
00:34:59,616 --> 00:35:00,517
[SPEAKER_00]: It makes sense.

415
00:35:00,537 --> 00:35:00,757
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

416
00:35:01,017 --> 00:35:01,297
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

417
00:35:01,497 --> 00:35:01,818
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

418
00:35:02,178 --> 00:35:03,039
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, he listens.

419
00:35:03,059 --> 00:35:03,659
[SPEAKER_01]: He hears this.

420
00:35:03,839 --> 00:35:04,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

421
00:35:05,140 --> 00:35:06,241
[SPEAKER_01]: This sounds promising.

422
00:35:06,682 --> 00:35:08,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Then he ponders on it.

423
00:35:08,223 --> 00:35:11,546
[SPEAKER_01]: He gets some understanding through reasoning and acceptance.

424
00:35:12,206 --> 00:35:12,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

425
00:35:12,827 --> 00:35:16,570
[SPEAKER_01]: And by having an understanding, that's already like almost like a beginning of wisdom.

426
00:35:17,782 --> 00:35:20,124
[SPEAKER_01]: By having that discernment, then he can...

427
00:35:20,144 --> 00:35:22,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so this is what needs to be done.

428
00:35:22,726 --> 00:35:32,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Because most of the people today, like monks or lay people, when it comes to practice, it's just this vast, ambiguous blur of everything and nothing.

429
00:35:33,054 --> 00:35:34,015
[SPEAKER_01]: What needs to be done?

430
00:35:34,035 --> 00:35:40,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you just, you know, be here, now, yesterday, tomorrow.

431
00:35:40,901 --> 00:35:42,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, as if you have a choice, but to be here now.

432
00:35:43,042 --> 00:35:43,943
[SPEAKER_01]: Where else can you be?

433
00:35:45,100 --> 00:35:48,561
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's just this ambiguous, weird, mystical... Why?

434
00:35:48,601 --> 00:35:50,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they haven't measured the task.

435
00:35:51,542 --> 00:35:56,363
[SPEAKER_01]: But you didn't assume this, and the Buddha would clearly say, and people understood, would clearly say, this needs to be done.

436
00:35:57,343 --> 00:35:58,083
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... and...

437
00:35:58,363 --> 00:36:03,585
[SPEAKER_01]: So, knowing what needs to be done, even it's patujana, requires some development of wisdom.

438
00:36:04,645 --> 00:36:10,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, measuring the task is actually quite a significant stage, so to speak.

439
00:36:10,286 --> 00:36:12,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Know, okay, if I do this,

440
00:36:13,723 --> 00:36:15,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I could expect that.

441
00:36:16,305 --> 00:36:29,172
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, if I were to apply myself in such a manner, for a certain period of time, I don't know exactly for how long, but if I keep applying myself in this manner, to that extent, I might be on the way to be free from suffering.

442
00:36:29,252 --> 00:36:30,013
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you do it.

443
00:36:30,453 --> 00:36:36,056
[SPEAKER_01]: And having it like that, measure the task, you won't need any external inspiration.

444
00:36:36,597 --> 00:36:37,157
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't need

445
00:36:38,413 --> 00:36:43,555
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you measure the task.

446
00:36:43,675 --> 00:36:46,256
[SPEAKER_01]: You see the end goal, you do it.

447
00:36:46,496 --> 00:36:47,537
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you do it.

448
00:36:48,057 --> 00:36:49,537
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you can't blame it.

449
00:36:49,597 --> 00:36:51,418
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't actually... you're beyond blaming.

450
00:36:52,072 --> 00:36:55,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Your circumstances...

451
00:36:55,134 --> 00:36:55,994
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes personal.

452
00:36:56,435 --> 00:37:06,901
[SPEAKER_01]: It becomes, like the Sutta said, to be known in the personal, individually, by the wise, by those who develop wisdom, to measure the task, to apply themself, and then to reach the goal.

453
00:37:07,401 --> 00:37:17,227
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like saying, uh, you see what's... Ah, there is nothing to be attained, there is nothing to be done, there is nothing... Find me the Sutta that says that.

454
00:37:17,947 --> 00:37:19,708
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't, because I read them all.

455
00:37:21,344 --> 00:37:24,845
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you'll find it in other schools of Buddhism and stuff.

456
00:37:25,045 --> 00:37:25,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And why?

457
00:37:25,546 --> 00:37:33,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they themselves haven't measured the task, haven't developed discernment to be able to have the tools to measure the task.

458
00:37:34,989 --> 00:37:38,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And all that's because they haven't been transparent from the beginning, self-transparent.

459
00:37:39,311 --> 00:37:46,894
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were, if you were transparent, you would always be coming from the place of, I want to be free from suffering, which means you'll never cover it up.

460
00:37:47,334 --> 00:37:50,515
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how pleasing the view is, how pleasing the teacher is,

461
00:37:51,517 --> 00:37:52,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this freeing me from suffering?

462
00:37:53,178 --> 00:37:53,558
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

463
00:37:53,678 --> 00:37:54,538
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll give it a bit more.

464
00:37:55,038 --> 00:37:55,919
[SPEAKER_01]: More time, more time.

465
00:37:56,279 --> 00:37:57,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're applying yourself.

466
00:37:58,259 --> 00:38:01,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's still not making a difference to your suffering, you will drop it.

467
00:38:01,440 --> 00:38:02,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You will move on.

468
00:38:03,241 --> 00:38:05,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that criteria is the fundament.

469
00:38:05,542 --> 00:38:09,343
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the criteria that, yes, that Patujana can trust.

470
00:38:10,564 --> 00:38:12,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, full transparent freedom from suffering.

471
00:38:13,705 --> 00:38:16,086
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, okay, this might be freeing me from suffering.

472
00:38:16,386 --> 00:38:17,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you stop and think.

473
00:38:19,087 --> 00:38:20,187
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody is to take away

474
00:38:20,673 --> 00:38:26,898
[SPEAKER_01]: This particular practice that I started engaging in, that I think is freeing me from suffering, would I suffer on account of that?

475
00:38:28,660 --> 00:38:29,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, I would.

476
00:38:30,001 --> 00:38:37,727
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you practice on that level even further, and then you'll see whether what you were doing was actually freeing you from suffering, or you were just holding on to it because it was pleasing to you.

477
00:38:40,409 --> 00:38:49,837
[SPEAKER_00]: But what if you, what if you, you... Do you want to check on them?

478
00:38:53,803 --> 00:39:01,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Or if you have a view that... a view that no one can take away, that's... that's the right view.

479
00:39:02,407 --> 00:39:07,370
[SPEAKER_00]: That no matter what way you think of it, well, you can't actually reverse it.

480
00:39:07,570 --> 00:39:10,091
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't see things in a different way.

481
00:39:10,311 --> 00:39:14,113
[SPEAKER_00]: If you... you... you could try... you would have to focus on... Not the exit, but doubt.

482
00:39:14,353 --> 00:39:14,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

483
00:39:15,814 --> 00:39:16,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is the right view.

484
00:39:17,254 --> 00:39:18,935
[SPEAKER_01]: When the facet of doubt disappears.

485
00:39:20,816 --> 00:39:28,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can reach the right view by fully, transparently doubting any views that you hold.

486
00:39:30,040 --> 00:39:33,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, don't hold back, don't leave anything unturned.

487
00:39:34,041 --> 00:39:39,143
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that which is left cannot be accessed by that full-on cultivated doubt.

488
00:39:42,704 --> 00:39:43,005
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

489
00:39:44,245 --> 00:39:49,227
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can see, like, you know, you can doubt this, you can doubt that, but you can't doubt

490
00:39:50,027 --> 00:39:56,834
[SPEAKER_01]: The presence of doubt, which means there is a there is a thing present, which means there is a nature of that thing.

491
00:39:58,055 --> 00:40:02,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And be it doubt or something else, you can't doubt the presence of thing.

492
00:40:02,540 --> 00:40:03,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what dhamma pertains to.

493
00:40:03,881 --> 00:40:04,642
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the beginning.

494
00:40:05,503 --> 00:40:11,609
[SPEAKER_01]: You take that course and then you'll be just kind of going further and further away from any doubt, in that sense.

495
00:40:16,023 --> 00:40:17,003
[SPEAKER_01]: There's happiness present.

496
00:40:17,504 --> 00:40:18,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's restlessness present.

497
00:40:19,265 --> 00:40:20,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's sensuality present.

498
00:40:21,086 --> 00:40:22,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, there's doubt present.

499
00:40:23,307 --> 00:40:24,708
[SPEAKER_01]: What all those things have in common?

500
00:40:25,588 --> 00:40:26,369
[SPEAKER_01]: A thing is present.

501
00:40:26,389 --> 00:40:26,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

502
00:40:27,410 --> 00:40:28,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's no doubt about that.

503
00:40:28,890 --> 00:40:31,232
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no doubt that can even access that.

504
00:40:32,397 --> 00:40:37,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can doubt particular aspects of an arising thing, but you can't doubt the arising of the thing.

505
00:40:37,680 --> 00:40:45,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why the insight of sotāpanna comes from that very popular... Whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing.

506
00:40:45,543 --> 00:40:49,065
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing.

507
00:40:49,145 --> 00:40:50,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't say this, which has the... No.

508
00:40:51,286 --> 00:40:59,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever, be it doubt, be it sensuality, be it happiness, be it sadness... Whatever has the nature of arising, for that reason,

509
00:40:59,800 --> 00:41:00,841
[SPEAKER_01]: As the nature of ceasing.

510
00:41:00,921 --> 00:41:03,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, for that reason, it's impermanent.

511
00:41:04,764 --> 00:41:05,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another topic.

512
00:41:05,704 --> 00:41:12,529
[SPEAKER_01]: I might have spoken about it in different talks as well, but things are not impermanent because you watch their destruction, watch their disappearance.

513
00:41:12,550 --> 00:41:14,611
[SPEAKER_01]: They're impermanent because they arose.

514
00:41:15,872 --> 00:41:16,733
[SPEAKER_01]: They came to be.

515
00:41:17,333 --> 00:41:18,834
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they're impermanent.

516
00:41:20,856 --> 00:41:22,237
[SPEAKER_00]: One could have...

517
00:41:22,277 --> 00:41:22,877
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what else to do.

518
00:41:23,037 --> 00:41:28,001
[SPEAKER_00]: One could have that view, or not doubt that, that view.

519
00:41:28,661 --> 00:41:29,242
[SPEAKER_00]: One could see it.

520
00:41:29,838 --> 00:41:43,945
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh, do you think it's possible to not have, eh... you're not necessarily released from dukkha?

521
00:41:44,945 --> 00:41:45,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

522
00:41:46,246 --> 00:41:46,846
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be.

523
00:41:46,986 --> 00:41:51,388
[SPEAKER_01]: It takes, it takes now repetition of that, that right view

524
00:41:52,202 --> 00:42:00,849
[SPEAKER_01]: On and on and on, until it kind of saturates your experience as a whole, and flushes out all the ignorance and avidya.

525
00:42:02,090 --> 00:42:04,031
[SPEAKER_00]: But you can still have no doubt, in a way.

526
00:42:04,872 --> 00:42:10,756
[SPEAKER_01]: You think that, like, no, you can have... Well, you can have no doubt, but whether... but, like, that's just the first step.

527
00:42:11,236 --> 00:42:15,179
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you have to keep applying that view, which is inaccessible to doubt.

528
00:42:15,860 --> 00:42:18,582
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not applying it, you're just holding that view, and you're not

529
00:42:19,343 --> 00:42:20,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like doing anything else.

530
00:42:21,104 --> 00:42:21,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

531
00:42:21,724 --> 00:42:24,665
[SPEAKER_01]: You go to the great view of... that's beyond the doubt.

532
00:42:24,905 --> 00:42:26,206
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not necessarily sotāpanna.

533
00:42:26,766 --> 00:42:36,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not necessarily... and that's why you have even those like dhamma-nusāri and sadhana-nusāri, those kind of putujanas, they were attained to the path, but they were not sotāpannas yet.

534
00:42:37,891 --> 00:42:47,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this sot... is this sot... dhamma-nusāri and sadhana-nusāri, whether they came through faith or they came through the dhamma investigation and reasoning.

535
00:42:48,434 --> 00:42:50,475
[SPEAKER_01]: They will arrive at the path eventually.

536
00:42:50,875 --> 00:42:52,516
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you can get the view.

537
00:42:52,656 --> 00:43:03,642
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, as I said, it's a gradual drilling, repetition, that it's sort of, like, bit by bit by bit by bit undoing your ignorance.

538
00:43:06,244 --> 00:43:12,888
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as the Buddha said, you can't say, oh, today my ignorance has been undone this much, yesterday that much, tomorrow it will be this much.

539
00:43:13,468 --> 00:43:17,190
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's like, like the carpenter using the axe every day for years,

540
00:43:19,033 --> 00:43:21,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Every day he grabs that axe.

541
00:43:21,574 --> 00:43:23,115
[SPEAKER_01]: The handle is wearing thin.

542
00:43:23,756 --> 00:43:28,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But he can't say, yesterday from today, oh, this is how much it wore off.

543
00:43:28,958 --> 00:43:34,801
[SPEAKER_01]: But after a year of using it, he can come and one day he can go, oh, my goodness, my axe handle is very thin.

544
00:43:35,082 --> 00:43:36,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, completely worn out.

545
00:43:41,765 --> 00:43:48,649
[SPEAKER_00]: Here is a person who is gone beyond doubt.

546
00:43:51,420 --> 00:44:13,629
[SPEAKER_00]: But that person would see, then, that it's... he can't doubt his view, but he's not getting any further on account of his own effort that he's making or not making, the amount of strength that he's putting into the practice or furthering their view or developing their view.

547
00:44:15,065 --> 00:44:23,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I mean, so, not only is you beyond doubt, but you're also beyond doubt that any further development is due to my own effort.

548
00:44:23,628 --> 00:44:24,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

549
00:44:25,128 --> 00:44:30,610
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, whatever was to be obtained by the manly eye for man is straight eye, I've obtained.

550
00:44:31,290 --> 00:44:32,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, it's completely on you.

551
00:44:34,211 --> 00:44:35,231
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you can't...

552
00:44:35,291 --> 00:44:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just others, or, like, people, and even other circumstances, it's irrelevant.

553
00:44:42,433 --> 00:44:44,774
[SPEAKER_01]: For as long as you have the living functioning mind,

554
00:44:47,168 --> 00:44:54,954
[SPEAKER_01]: As you say, once you get the right view, there is nothing standing in between you and Arahantship, except your lack of effort.

555
00:44:56,736 --> 00:44:57,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

556
00:44:57,736 --> 00:45:05,523
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing else that can come in the way, which is why it can't be taken away, because it wasn't given to you externally either.

557
00:45:05,543 --> 00:45:11,127
[SPEAKER_01]: It's something you developed fully, and it's like a one-way, one-way development.

558
00:45:11,307 --> 00:45:12,148
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't undo it.

559
00:45:15,663 --> 00:45:19,345
[SPEAKER_01]: But nothing except yourself stands in the way, in that sense.

560
00:45:26,630 --> 00:45:27,771
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

561
00:45:28,051 --> 00:45:29,052
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't remember this quote.

562
00:45:30,052 --> 00:45:30,973
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a Stoic thing.

563
00:45:31,333 --> 00:45:42,080
[SPEAKER_00]: It says that a God is made free from all troubles on account of nature.

564
00:45:45,405 --> 00:45:49,846
[SPEAKER_00]: But the sage is free from all trouble.

565
00:45:49,866 --> 00:45:50,727
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of his own effort.

566
00:45:50,767 --> 00:45:51,947
[SPEAKER_00]: On account of his own effort.

567
00:45:52,727 --> 00:45:53,788
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's better.

568
00:45:54,168 --> 00:45:55,228
[SPEAKER_00]: He's even stronger than the God.

569
00:45:55,248 --> 00:45:55,888
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

570
00:45:55,908 --> 00:46:00,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he's not... Well, exactly, he's stronger than the God, because God was not strong, because they were given.

571
00:46:00,749 --> 00:46:01,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because they were just given to him.

572
00:46:02,010 --> 00:46:02,530
[SPEAKER_01]: You were given.

573
00:46:02,670 --> 00:46:03,730
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, you took it for granted.

574
00:46:04,850 --> 00:46:06,151
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't have to develop anything.

575
00:46:06,611 --> 00:46:06,851
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

576
00:46:10,492 --> 00:46:11,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

577
00:46:23,093 --> 00:46:30,238
[SPEAKER_00]: We're seeing the senses again, as external, external things.

578
00:46:30,898 --> 00:46:36,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if I think about my eye, I see that as an external thought.

579
00:46:37,302 --> 00:46:38,843
[SPEAKER_00]: But my eye says she...

580
00:46:41,405 --> 00:46:52,392
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem there is that in regard to that thought, you're having like a tacit, implicit assumption that the eye is something more.

581
00:46:53,503 --> 00:46:54,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Internally.

582
00:46:55,283 --> 00:47:04,025
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can think your thoughts, I think, while you at the same time assume to find your eye in the direction of internal, inside of you, or whatever.

583
00:47:04,425 --> 00:47:12,507
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at yourself in the mirror, that eye that you see in the mirror, that is that eye because of which you're able to look at that eye in the mirror in the first place.

584
00:47:12,747 --> 00:47:17,468
[SPEAKER_01]: That very reflection there, of that fleshy organ, is that object

585
00:47:18,001 --> 00:47:19,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're saying it doesn't go further than what...

586
00:47:19,882 --> 00:47:21,443
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no further than that.

587
00:47:21,843 --> 00:47:23,023
[SPEAKER_01]: It closes upon itself.

588
00:47:23,344 --> 00:47:31,648
[SPEAKER_01]: The assumption takes you further into infinity, whereby you would see the reflection of yourself, seeing the reflection of yourself, seeing the reflection, and think, wow!

589
00:47:32,428 --> 00:47:38,991
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, all the whole thing would be swept in a second, if you remove the flesh you are, if you destroy it.

590
00:47:39,792 --> 00:47:43,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole infinity of your perception, whatever else, it's impermanent.

591
00:47:44,194 --> 00:47:46,375
[SPEAKER_01]: It might be infinite, but it doesn't last forever.

592
00:47:48,534 --> 00:47:48,754
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

593
00:47:48,874 --> 00:47:50,995
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you flash your eyes, gone.

594
00:47:51,515 --> 00:47:54,236
[SPEAKER_01]: Accident, death, sickness, aging, whatever, gone.

595
00:47:55,076 --> 00:47:56,157
[SPEAKER_00]: The whole infinity is gone.

596
00:47:57,357 --> 00:47:58,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Your sight is gone.

597
00:47:59,338 --> 00:47:59,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

598
00:47:59,738 --> 00:48:05,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that flashed your eye was that because of which the whole domain of sight was present.

599
00:48:07,100 --> 00:48:13,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why that's... and you'll never see like a one, two, three, four, five, a linear connection.

600
00:48:14,023 --> 00:48:17,284
[SPEAKER_01]: So you have to contemplate, you have to fabricate it.

601
00:48:17,782 --> 00:48:21,043
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, it's abstract, but that's only because it doesn't apply to your experience.

602
00:48:21,143 --> 00:48:26,745
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to make it apply to your experience, whereby you have to drill in, on and on and on.

603
00:48:27,386 --> 00:48:29,386
[SPEAKER_01]: Drill in a thought, literally.

604
00:48:30,767 --> 00:48:37,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Estab... like... define it, think it, and then establish it.

605
00:48:37,750 --> 00:48:46,953
[SPEAKER_01]: The thought that those fleshy organs are that because of which I am alive here, thinking, intending, looking, smelling, touching.

606
00:48:47,732 --> 00:48:49,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Those fleshy organs disappear.

607
00:48:50,194 --> 00:48:54,036
[SPEAKER_01]: All of this cannot remain standing even for a split second longer.

608
00:48:56,377 --> 00:48:59,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And in the beginning, it's an abstract thought, but you keep drilling it.

609
00:49:00,220 --> 00:49:02,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it will stay longer and longer and longer.

610
00:49:02,941 --> 00:49:06,603
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when it stays long enough, you start seeing things through that thought.

611
00:49:07,404 --> 00:49:11,887
[SPEAKER_01]: You start seeing things through the right view, when it takes root.

612
00:49:13,968 --> 00:49:17,250
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say those senses, those senses are

613
00:49:19,446 --> 00:49:20,947
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing more than what I experienced now.

614
00:49:22,609 --> 00:49:25,591
[SPEAKER_00]: That is, that is the senses.

615
00:49:25,871 --> 00:49:26,091
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

616
00:49:26,371 --> 00:49:29,974
[SPEAKER_00]: What I say, the flesh organ, that, what I think, that is it.

617
00:49:31,516 --> 00:49:38,721
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something you also see through your senses, but that you can know that is that, because of which your senses are there in the first place.

618
00:49:38,861 --> 00:49:45,927
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of turning... Well, it's, yeah, yeah, it's like a, like, kind of closes upon itself, so to speak.

619
00:49:51,097 --> 00:49:54,098
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't go... it's not anywhere else.

620
00:49:54,338 --> 00:49:54,518
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

621
00:49:54,698 --> 00:49:55,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Other than...

622
00:49:55,678 --> 00:49:56,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Right here, right now.

623
00:50:00,259 --> 00:50:10,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, look yourself in the mirror, look at your eyes in the mirror, and you'll see how you assume, like, as if you were behind them, inside, somewhere, left or right.

624
00:50:12,161 --> 00:50:19,942
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you then don't let your thought, or rather if you anchor your thought in that reflection of that fleshy organ,

625
00:50:20,697 --> 00:50:43,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you look yourself in the mirror, and you'll see your eyes, and then you can, okay, this whole, like, the face, and the flesh, and the nose, and stuff, you can kind of see that, okay, that's just organs.

626
00:50:43,879 --> 00:50:49,584
[SPEAKER_01]: But pin it down to the iris in your eyes, and you kind of think the life is behind that, i.e.

627
00:50:49,604 --> 00:50:50,465
[SPEAKER_01]: your sense of self.

628
00:50:50,565 --> 00:50:50,985
[SPEAKER_01]: You are.

629
00:50:51,666 --> 00:50:59,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at it, what you see, you see a black circle, a black dot, in the center of that flesh you're holding.

630
00:51:00,033 --> 00:51:01,374
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, that's it.

631
00:51:01,675 --> 00:51:02,335
[SPEAKER_01]: It stops there.

632
00:51:02,475 --> 00:51:03,036
[SPEAKER_01]: It starts there.

633
00:51:03,056 --> 00:51:03,876
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't go beyond that.

634
00:51:04,057 --> 00:51:05,458
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that flesh that I'm looking at.

635
00:51:05,998 --> 00:51:08,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that because of which I'm able to look?

636
00:51:09,887 --> 00:51:12,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there are like two dimensions simultaneously present.

637
00:51:12,590 --> 00:51:19,618
[SPEAKER_01]: One is this material dimension of a fleshy eye, and the one is like simultaneously present dimension of awareness in regard to it.

638
00:51:21,380 --> 00:51:28,067
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is not like a linear that behind the eyes, it goes and you look, you know, eyes are like the window into the soul.

639
00:51:28,507 --> 00:51:28,968
[SPEAKER_01]: They aren't.

640
00:51:29,488 --> 00:51:30,630
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just a flat wall.

641
00:51:34,188 --> 00:51:36,049
[SPEAKER_01]: The soul is in front of it.

642
00:51:37,109 --> 00:51:38,170
[SPEAKER_01]: The thoughts are in front of it.

643
00:51:38,190 --> 00:51:39,450
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't access that matter.

644
00:51:39,490 --> 00:51:40,291
[SPEAKER_01]: They can't enter it.

645
00:51:41,391 --> 00:51:42,432
[SPEAKER_01]: They separate the plane.

646
00:51:46,414 --> 00:51:52,237
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's that assumption of that quick eye movement that makes you, well, makes you maintain that assumption.

647
00:51:52,797 --> 00:51:54,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, it's not the assumption of quick eye.

648
00:51:54,698 --> 00:51:55,798
[SPEAKER_01]: The eyes move fast.

649
00:51:56,339 --> 00:51:59,380
[SPEAKER_01]: It makes you, like, helps you maintain that assumption easily.

650
00:52:00,301 --> 00:52:00,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, the

651
00:52:01,372 --> 00:52:03,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Your intentionality is measured by the movement.

652
00:52:04,453 --> 00:52:07,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Things you do, things you intend with your body, with your speech, with your mind.

653
00:52:08,615 --> 00:52:10,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Say your whole body is paralyzed.

654
00:52:10,576 --> 00:52:12,557
[SPEAKER_01]: You can always, like, move the eyes.

655
00:52:12,657 --> 00:52:15,298
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically when the eyes are motionless, well, it's dead.

656
00:52:15,738 --> 00:52:16,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eyes are dead.

657
00:52:19,700 --> 00:52:25,683
[SPEAKER_01]: But even those eyes, the quickest moving, it's still just flesh yoga.

658
00:52:25,903 --> 00:52:28,364
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, your hand is, your nails are, and your hair is.

659
00:52:30,228 --> 00:52:37,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Your spectacles are, your robes are flesh, material, matter, rūpa.

660
00:52:38,695 --> 00:52:45,581
[SPEAKER_01]: But this matter is there because of which there is that awareness in regard to it anyway, in the first place.

661
00:52:47,623 --> 00:52:49,885
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's just like two completely separate

662
00:52:51,480 --> 00:52:58,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Domains of matter and that awareness in regard to the matter inseparable from each other.

663
00:52:58,184 --> 00:52:59,365
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have one without the other.

664
00:52:59,445 --> 00:53:00,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Inconceivable.

665
00:53:00,265 --> 00:53:05,689
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just think matter, because, as I said, you're thinking matter, which means there's awareness in regard to matter.

666
00:53:06,429 --> 00:53:09,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor can you just have pure awareness, which is ridiculous.

667
00:53:10,020 --> 00:53:12,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you have pure awareness if your method is destroyed?

668
00:53:13,002 --> 00:53:13,423
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

669
00:53:13,463 --> 00:53:13,903
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

670
00:53:14,003 --> 00:53:16,845
[SPEAKER_01]: You can assume that there is a pure awareness, the divine, whatever else.

671
00:53:17,145 --> 00:53:17,546
[SPEAKER_01]: There isn't.

672
00:53:17,946 --> 00:53:20,488
[SPEAKER_01]: But you see the truth about your body, yourself, too.

673
00:53:20,548 --> 00:53:36,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Or certainly, you... it will always remain an assumption, because you can never find out that there is a... that there is an assumption... oh, sorry, an awareness free from your... free from the material aspect of life of your body.

674
00:53:37,161 --> 00:53:37,541
[SPEAKER_01]: There isn't.

675
00:53:39,509 --> 00:54:05,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in good faith, that is, because you can't, if you look at it, if you look at your experience as a whole, and even if you conceive it, no matter how pleasing that conception is, that your awareness can be separate, the very reality of your practice, of the present moment, shows you that, well, I'm thinking that my awareness is separate, while I'm all determined by this matter, thus I'm not separate.

676
00:54:08,019 --> 00:54:09,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only assume it.

677
00:54:09,640 --> 00:54:16,862
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you can only assume the matter as independent of you, because that's the thought, which is you, in that sense.

678
00:54:17,382 --> 00:54:19,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why matter finds footing.

679
00:54:25,765 --> 00:54:30,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It finds footing, basically, in your experience, because you provided, you assumed it.

680
00:54:32,168 --> 00:54:35,569
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you see the flesh here, I... You're saying pure matter.

681
00:54:36,099 --> 00:54:40,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the four great elements find footing in how you conceive the existence of matter.

682
00:54:42,443 --> 00:54:43,904
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you assume the eyes are yours.

683
00:54:43,924 --> 00:54:45,645
[SPEAKER_01]: It means those eyes have found a footing.

684
00:54:45,665 --> 00:54:48,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Those fleshy lumps organs have found a footing.

685
00:54:49,727 --> 00:54:50,568
[SPEAKER_01]: They exist for you.

686
00:54:50,688 --> 00:54:51,248
[SPEAKER_01]: They're mine.

687
00:54:54,270 --> 00:55:04,076
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, all you're going to see are fleshy eyes moving left and right, whatever, with a black iris in the center.

688
00:55:05,138 --> 00:55:06,839
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is out there.

689
00:55:07,440 --> 00:55:08,160
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing.

690
00:55:08,300 --> 00:55:13,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you go through that black iris, you just find, you know, blood and liquid and whatever else eyes have.

691
00:55:16,946 --> 00:55:20,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no... there is that direction of matter and that's it.

692
00:55:22,009 --> 00:55:23,410
[SPEAKER_01]: You drill further, you'll find matter.

693
00:55:23,790 --> 00:55:25,632
[SPEAKER_01]: More matter.

694
00:55:25,732 --> 00:55:28,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Or the same matter, depending how generally you look at it.

695
00:55:28,814 --> 00:55:33,757
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you have to do is stop assuming that you are behind that matter.

696
00:55:35,277 --> 00:55:42,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can stop assuming that you're behind that matter if you drill long enough, thought, that you are not behind that matter.

697
00:55:43,284 --> 00:55:46,708
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to keep drilling and drilling it and drilling it until it takes root.

698
00:55:50,972 --> 00:55:57,718
[SPEAKER_01]: And when it does, then seeing your own eyes, your own movement, your own senses, will not induce that assumption of self behind those senses.

699
00:56:21,788 --> 00:56:28,192
[SPEAKER_00]: We spoke about, I don't know, non-descending into the mouth.

700
00:56:30,674 --> 00:56:34,377
[SPEAKER_00]: That's, you know, that's a whole... Not descending into your eyes.

701
00:56:34,937 --> 00:56:35,198
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

702
00:56:35,538 --> 00:56:36,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Not descending into your eyes.

703
00:56:36,619 --> 00:56:38,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Not descending into matter.

704
00:56:38,640 --> 00:56:40,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Not taking up matter.

705
00:56:41,382 --> 00:56:42,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Not assuming it.

706
00:56:43,284 --> 00:56:44,865
[SPEAKER_01]: But in more practical terms, yeah.

707
00:56:45,846 --> 00:56:47,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Being mindful of your body as a whole.

708
00:56:48,031 --> 00:56:57,575
[SPEAKER_01]: means your mind won't descend into it, so to speak, whereby you'll end up acting, performing bodily acts carelessly, not descending into your mouth.

709
00:56:57,915 --> 00:57:04,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, your mind not descending into your mouth will make you aware of what you're saying and what is coming out of your mouth.

710
00:57:04,978 --> 00:57:06,538
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it won't make you act carelessly.

711
00:57:07,178 --> 00:57:11,300
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, not having your citta descend, like, in your own mind,

712
00:57:13,442 --> 00:57:19,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't get complacent there, which means that even your thoughts you'll be aware of and you won't be thinking carelessly.

713
00:57:22,204 --> 00:57:39,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't descend or mindful in a proper way of your mind, as an external, seeing it externally, in a proper way,

714
00:57:44,062 --> 00:57:47,365
[SPEAKER_00]: Just holding that where you're seeing.

715
00:57:50,127 --> 00:57:53,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Then we can start talking about jhānas.

716
00:57:53,049 --> 00:57:55,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, that's... Yeah, yeah.

717
00:57:55,851 --> 00:58:10,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you make a practice out of it, out of establishing your mind, well, upon the certain things, like thinking and pondering, overcoming the speech determination and so on, then, yeah.

718
00:58:10,603 --> 00:58:13,285
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are many different ways of establishing your mind.

719
00:58:13,750 --> 00:58:14,871
[SPEAKER_01]: Jhānas are just one of them.

720
00:58:16,071 --> 00:58:18,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, establish your mind upon the perception of forest.

721
00:58:18,933 --> 00:58:29,559
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you go into town, none of the perceptions that come from the town can access you, or bother you, or cause you harm, because your mind is established upon the great perception of the forest.

722
00:58:30,100 --> 00:58:42,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you recognize, okay, so I'm free from the disturbances that come based on the perceptions that... from the town, but if there are perceptions that... if there are disturbances coming on account of perceptions in the forest, I will be disturbed.

723
00:58:43,152 --> 00:58:49,617
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why don't I develop my mind, or rather establish my mind upon something even greater, so that then even the forest cannot disturb me?

724
00:58:50,177 --> 00:58:52,679
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you can establish your mind upon the perception of earth.

725
00:58:53,680 --> 00:59:03,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But establishing your mind on the perception of earth doesn't mean actually holding the perception of earth in front of you?

726
00:59:03,947 --> 00:59:04,268
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

727
00:59:04,508 --> 00:59:04,668
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

728
00:59:04,888 --> 00:59:08,711
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically finding it, like discerning it in things.

729
00:59:09,376 --> 00:59:12,397
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you don't need to hold it, because it's already implied in those things.

730
00:59:12,737 --> 00:59:13,957
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not fabricating anything.

731
00:59:13,977 --> 00:59:14,717
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not creating it.

732
00:59:15,417 --> 00:59:30,861
[SPEAKER_01]: The most obvious, maybe a bit crude example, would be, well, what does town, and forest, and the mountains, and the oceans, for that matter, have in common?

733
00:59:31,781 --> 00:59:32,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yes.

734
00:59:32,922 --> 00:59:34,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's an arisen thing.

735
00:59:35,942 --> 00:59:37,003
[SPEAKER_01]: What is that arisen thing?

736
00:59:37,023 --> 00:59:38,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Those arisen things, what do they have in common?

737
00:59:39,309 --> 00:59:47,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, they do have in common the fact that they're on Earth, that they're Earth, they're part of Earth, that they have the solid aspect.

738
00:59:50,452 --> 01:00:00,175
[SPEAKER_01]: That phenomenon of solidity, or immovability, or Earth, you want to call it, you establish your mind upon it.

739
01:00:01,736 --> 01:00:04,737
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an abstraction now, because your mind is not established.

740
01:00:05,617 --> 01:00:08,098
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have it at the background of your mind,

741
01:00:09,566 --> 01:00:17,550
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, anchored in the notion of solid in everything, even the running water, your mind will get established.

742
01:00:17,570 --> 01:00:20,711
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the casinos work, in the same sense.

743
01:00:20,731 --> 01:00:21,752
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what casinos are.

744
01:00:22,892 --> 01:00:24,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would establish your mind in...

745
01:00:25,073 --> 01:00:36,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing that comes on a particular level can disturb you, because your mind has been established to that much greater element of solidity in everything particular that you experience.

746
01:00:37,259 --> 01:00:38,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you literally surpass

747
01:00:39,195 --> 01:00:43,758
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole domain of particular things, which is everything, or whatever.

748
01:00:43,878 --> 01:01:05,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you... if you... about the thought, like you have a... once you establish your mind on thought, general thought, so any thought that you have, or you establish your mind on mind... Well, that would be the casino of consciousness, which is the most refined of all of them, and it would come kind of last.

749
01:01:07,476 --> 01:01:21,989
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, you can just establish your mind upon the general experience as a whole, right here, right now, but make sure that it's not divorced from that material aspect, the material end of it, so to speak.

750
01:01:23,691 --> 01:01:28,896
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, if you come from the material end of it, you make sure you don't overlook that.

751
01:01:31,138 --> 01:01:32,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are here now,

752
01:01:35,294 --> 01:01:47,243
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is any disturbance to arise, it will be on account of your presence here, on the hill, in the central Sri Lanka, in this area, or whatever.

753
01:01:47,924 --> 01:01:55,650
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, now you have, like, you know, phones, emails, stuff like that, so disturbance cannot...

754
01:01:55,810 --> 01:01:59,372
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, even that would arise through your current situation here.

755
01:02:01,074 --> 01:02:02,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, on account of your senses.

756
01:02:03,216 --> 01:02:07,419
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why there are many different ways of establishing your mind, but it's the same principle of establishing your mind.

757
01:02:08,200 --> 01:02:10,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha, when he spoke about the casinos,

758
01:02:26,830 --> 01:02:33,974
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of that, he abandons everything inferior to it.

759
01:02:34,094 --> 01:02:36,596
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is inferior to the phenomenon of earth?

760
01:02:37,656 --> 01:02:40,878
[SPEAKER_01]: That thought of earth, general earth?

761
01:02:41,318 --> 01:02:46,101
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, anything more particular than that is inferior to that phenomenon of earth.

762
01:02:47,361 --> 01:02:50,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he said, but obviously, being

763
01:02:50,965 --> 01:02:54,447
[SPEAKER_01]: He's still liable to disturbance on account of the general phenomenon.

764
01:02:54,467 --> 01:03:01,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, it's much less disturbance than the particular disturbances that can arise if you're not established upon the element of Earth.

765
01:03:02,771 --> 01:03:11,015
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, Tathāgata has seen the gratification, the danger in this case, in regard to the casino of Earth.

766
01:03:11,936 --> 01:03:15,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing that, he knew it, and he went beyond through that knowledge.

767
01:03:15,357 --> 01:03:16,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's not bothered by it either.

768
01:03:18,262 --> 01:03:29,325
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there is establishing mind upon a casino, or whatever, and then there is knowing that as well.

769
01:03:30,125 --> 01:03:30,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Stay in terms of this.

770
01:03:31,266 --> 01:03:33,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, it's not descending into the thing.

771
01:03:34,486 --> 01:03:41,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not, yeah, not lapsing mindfulness, or, like, and lapsing mindfulness doesn't mean, like, you know, you need to constantly keep your mind in front of you.

772
01:03:41,928 --> 01:03:43,629
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it means you need to anchor your mind.

773
01:03:44,209 --> 01:03:47,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can, whether you're talking now, sit

774
01:03:48,984 --> 01:03:53,207
[SPEAKER_01]: Standing, walking, any body posture, means the body's there.

775
01:03:54,689 --> 01:03:57,991
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, the earth element is there.

776
01:03:59,272 --> 01:04:01,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, the water element is there.

777
01:04:01,714 --> 01:04:03,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, the fire element is there.

778
01:04:03,055 --> 01:04:04,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, the air element is there.

779
01:04:04,657 --> 01:04:05,477
[SPEAKER_01]: You can take any of it.

780
01:04:06,178 --> 01:04:11,062
[SPEAKER_01]: They're kind of slightly more refined, but you can just take your thought element, yeah?

781
01:04:11,282 --> 01:04:13,023
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even take the elements, just take the body.

782
01:04:13,183 --> 01:04:13,944
[SPEAKER_01]: The body's there.

783
01:04:14,204 --> 01:04:14,685
[SPEAKER_01]: That's enough.

784
01:04:15,955 --> 01:04:17,076
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't even have to take the body.

785
01:04:17,096 --> 01:04:19,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Just take... this current posture is there.

786
01:04:19,077 --> 01:04:21,158
[SPEAKER_01]: When that posture changes, take that posture is there.

787
01:04:21,758 --> 01:04:22,539
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the thing.

788
01:04:22,799 --> 01:04:28,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever disturbances can enter you right now, it would be while you're sitting.

789
01:04:29,803 --> 01:04:33,925
[SPEAKER_01]: It would be while your body is seated, the senses are liable to such and such.

790
01:04:34,285 --> 01:04:35,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So you anchor your mind

791
01:04:39,080 --> 01:04:40,561
[SPEAKER_01]: in the seated position of your body.

792
01:04:40,621 --> 01:04:42,042
[SPEAKER_01]: When you get up, you anchor your mind.

793
01:04:42,342 --> 01:04:46,165
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, you anchor your mind in the notion of the body is there.

794
01:04:47,205 --> 01:04:49,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And obviously, if you're thinking it in front of you, you're not anchored.

795
01:04:51,108 --> 01:04:52,629
[SPEAKER_01]: You're anchored when you stop thinking it.

796
01:04:53,649 --> 01:04:54,790
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're just discerning it.

797
01:04:54,850 --> 01:04:57,192
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're just seeing it in the background of your mind.

798
01:04:58,212 --> 01:05:00,214
[SPEAKER_01]: That... that's what means being anchored.

799
01:05:00,794 --> 01:05:03,996
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, you throw an anchor and then you forget about it, because you're anchored.

800
01:05:04,597 --> 01:05:05,777
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know that it's there.

801
01:05:06,358 --> 01:05:08,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep pulling it out and throwing it.

802
01:05:08,858 --> 01:05:11,739
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep holding on to it, because it's anchored.

803
01:05:12,240 --> 01:05:12,800
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, leave.

804
01:05:12,840 --> 01:05:15,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Let the boat float, left and right, wherever it wants to go.

805
01:05:16,402 --> 01:05:18,963
[SPEAKER_01]: If it goes too far and starts dragging the anchor, i.e.

806
01:05:19,103 --> 01:05:22,344
[SPEAKER_01]: if it ceases to be anchored, then restart it.

807
01:05:22,384 --> 01:05:23,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Re-establish your mind.

808
01:05:24,445 --> 01:05:28,007
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not talking about for, like, sitting, you know, cross-legged and...

809
01:05:29,388 --> 01:05:31,709
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking about, like, daily life.

810
01:05:32,350 --> 01:05:33,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Anchor your mind in your date.

811
01:05:33,611 --> 01:05:35,913
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how... that's what establishing your mind is.

812
01:05:35,933 --> 01:05:38,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, he enters and abides the first jhāna.

813
01:05:38,715 --> 01:05:39,996
[SPEAKER_01]: He enters and abides this.

814
01:05:40,456 --> 01:05:53,546
[SPEAKER_01]: He establishes his mind and, like, enters and abides the perceptions of forest, and then he goes to the town for pitta pāda or whatever else, and he's not bothered by anything in that town, because his mind is still fully anchored in the perceptions of the forest.

815
01:05:54,267 --> 01:06:00,632
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's no question of acting and doing stuff while you're established, because that's how it works.

816
01:06:01,342 --> 01:06:06,204
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what establishment means.

817
01:06:06,224 --> 01:06:11,887
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, oh, I can establish my mind only when I don't do anything, lock the door and just sit and stare at a wall.

818
01:06:14,248 --> 01:06:14,508
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

819
01:06:17,889 --> 01:06:20,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's discerning, the more general.

820
01:06:22,311 --> 01:06:27,974
[SPEAKER_01]: So whether you're here or there or here and there, that means your body has to be there.

821
01:06:28,694 --> 01:06:30,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Have a notion of your body

822
01:06:31,824 --> 01:06:37,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Let it be as remote as it wants to be, but have it present in the back of your mind while you're doing other things.

823
01:06:38,086 --> 01:06:38,926
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the important bit.

824
01:06:39,066 --> 01:06:39,486
[SPEAKER_01]: While.

825
01:06:41,307 --> 01:06:44,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how we can discern this principle of simultaneous presence.

826
01:06:45,308 --> 01:06:47,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, I'll stop this because I'm doing that.

827
01:06:51,790 --> 01:06:53,330
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop only on wholesome stuff.

828
01:06:54,911 --> 01:06:57,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that obviously undermines the whole picture.

829
01:06:58,272 --> 01:06:59,792
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, while you're anchored,

830
01:07:01,525 --> 01:07:03,867
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even if you don't do anything, that's a form of doing.

831
01:07:04,928 --> 01:07:05,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

832
01:07:05,789 --> 01:07:08,591
[SPEAKER_01]: I am refraining from activity which is my current activity.

833
01:07:10,572 --> 01:07:17,078
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while my mind is active, established in the presence of the body there, I'm refraining from any particular activity.

834
01:07:18,239 --> 01:07:18,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Fine.

835
01:07:19,239 --> 01:07:20,000
[SPEAKER_01]: As long as you're mindful.

836
01:07:20,660 --> 01:07:21,701
[SPEAKER_01]: In that correct sense.

837
01:07:21,942 --> 01:07:27,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Not mindful like in a, you know, like modern psychology sense or whatever, new age sense.

838
01:07:27,546 --> 01:07:28,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, just be mindful.

839
01:07:29,528 --> 01:07:31,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness needs to be understood.

840
01:07:31,610 --> 01:07:34,372
[SPEAKER_01]: The principle of anchoring needs to be... that's satipaṭṭhāna.

841
01:07:34,972 --> 01:07:36,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And for that, you need the right view.

842
01:07:45,260 --> 01:07:58,789
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you're sitting here, thinking, trying to formulate the next question, or the same, or the clarification, you can still have a vague, distant, ambiguous notion of your body present at the back of your mind.

843
01:07:59,750 --> 01:08:01,311
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that's practice of anchoring.

844
01:08:02,420 --> 01:08:06,561
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't require the direct effort, because if it's a direct effort, it means it's not anchoring.

845
01:08:07,041 --> 01:08:18,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you start with a direct effort, only to leave it kind of seep in the background, without losing sight of it.

846
01:08:18,863 --> 01:08:28,185
[SPEAKER_01]: And eventually, it becomes effortless, because you can't lose the sight of it, because the particular instances of this body are maintained in the nature of bodies there.

847
01:08:30,439 --> 01:08:31,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's right there in front of you.

848
01:08:32,480 --> 01:08:37,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Anyhow, the earth element is right there for as long as you're alive.

849
01:08:38,383 --> 01:08:39,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to keep thinking about it.

850
01:08:42,385 --> 01:08:44,987
[SPEAKER_01]: But you need to let it go in the background and then discern it there.

851
01:08:45,007 --> 01:08:48,228
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you discern it there, then, well, that kind of becomes a foreground almost.

852
01:08:48,408 --> 01:08:49,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mind is established.

853
01:08:58,023 --> 01:08:58,984
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the boundary.

854
01:08:59,264 --> 01:09:00,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneous presence.

855
01:09:00,304 --> 01:09:01,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the boundary of things.

856
01:09:02,566 --> 01:09:03,006
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean?

857
01:09:03,587 --> 01:09:07,869
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the body and everything else that's happening.

858
01:09:09,050 --> 01:09:10,331
[SPEAKER_01]: No, but what do you mean by boundary?

859
01:09:11,232 --> 01:09:12,212
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's the extent.

860
01:09:13,313 --> 01:09:14,293
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, the extent of it.

861
01:09:14,574 --> 01:09:14,814
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

862
01:09:15,274 --> 01:09:24,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, whatever else is happening, whatever else happened, whatever might happen, it will happen for as long as... or on account of the presence of the body right here, right there, right now.

863
01:09:24,660 --> 01:09:27,382
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't... so then you don't... Then you don't chase after it.

864
01:09:27,926 --> 01:09:30,047
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to, oh, well, how did this happen in the past?

865
01:09:30,067 --> 01:09:30,887
[SPEAKER_01]: How did all of this happen?

866
01:09:31,268 --> 01:09:36,950
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you know, for as long as... That's where, on the other hand, we kind of completely remove the ignorance whatsoever.

867
01:09:36,970 --> 01:09:45,755
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, any disturbances that are left are on account of this, like, living body right here, until that body breaks apart, and then even that won't be.

868
01:09:46,375 --> 01:09:52,718
[SPEAKER_01]: But that disturbance for him is just a disturbance of, you know, pain, enduring, and so on.

869
01:09:52,758 --> 01:09:55,640
[SPEAKER_01]: Not disturbance in terms of causing him suffering and dukkha.

870
01:09:55,700 --> 01:09:56,440
[SPEAKER_01]: He's beyond that.

871
01:09:57,072 --> 01:09:57,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a nuisance.

872
01:09:58,333 --> 01:10:02,136
[SPEAKER_01]: Any nuisance that was left, it's on account of this body for as long as it's functioning.

873
01:10:02,596 --> 01:10:04,037
[SPEAKER_01]: When that breaks apart, that's it.

874
01:10:04,997 --> 01:10:06,418
[SPEAKER_01]: There won't be another body taken up.

875
01:10:12,943 --> 01:10:20,728
[SPEAKER_00]: The thing is, eh... things like, eh... if one does the practice correctly, well, there's no assumption.

876
01:10:21,529 --> 01:10:25,992
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no assuming, eh... the senses.

877
01:10:28,656 --> 01:10:33,279
[SPEAKER_00]: You're practicing in a way, you're not so... mindful, it's not becoming confused.

878
01:10:40,725 --> 01:10:42,246
[SPEAKER_00]: You could experience nibbāna.

879
01:10:43,447 --> 01:10:45,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

880
01:10:45,288 --> 01:10:46,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Sotāpanna experienced nibbāna.

881
01:10:47,390 --> 01:10:48,350
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's not established.

882
01:10:48,370 --> 01:10:49,691
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not established, exactly.

883
01:10:50,652 --> 01:10:50,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

884
01:10:51,213 --> 01:10:54,235
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, kutujanas can experience nibbāna.

885
01:10:55,075 --> 01:10:57,297
[SPEAKER_01]: But because they're kutujanas, they won't be a nibbāna.

886
01:10:58,474 --> 01:11:00,896
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of the experience, it is that which Nibbāna is.

887
01:11:01,597 --> 01:11:02,037
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

888
01:11:02,057 --> 01:11:15,027
[SPEAKER_01]: Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārāja Mahārā

889
01:11:19,706 --> 01:11:24,609
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he goes to the point where he says, he thinks, I am... this is nibbāna.

890
01:11:25,129 --> 01:11:26,070
[SPEAKER_01]: I am in nibbāna.

891
01:11:26,410 --> 01:11:28,551
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm entering nibbāna with nibbāna's mind.

892
01:11:29,511 --> 01:11:34,414
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, although that is actually a nibbāna, what he's talking about.

893
01:11:34,854 --> 01:11:41,317
[SPEAKER_01]: He is not established on it, nor discerning, nor having it developed, because he's still thinking, I am.

894
01:11:42,198 --> 01:11:46,140
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nibbāna is just basically experience without greed, aversion, delusion.

895
01:11:47,255 --> 01:12:06,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Imagine if you establish yourself in that 24-7, until it takes its roots, and then it just goes off on its own, and you can drop the whole thing, and there is absolutely no cracks in the wall anymore, that nothing can enter you.

896
01:12:06,340 --> 01:12:07,040
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just done.

897
01:12:08,441 --> 01:12:09,341
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what nibbāna is.

898
01:12:10,922 --> 01:12:11,742
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sometimes

899
01:12:12,973 --> 01:12:15,794
[SPEAKER_01]: All the Buddha would give would be the general instruction.

900
01:12:15,974 --> 01:12:18,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, abstain from anything unwholesome.

901
01:12:18,696 --> 01:12:19,556
